==English==
{{was wotd|2009|January|11}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|ML.|āctuātus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|āctuō||actuate, implement}}, from {{der|en|la|āctus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|agō||do, act}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈæktʃu.eɪt/|/ˈæktju.eɪt/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈæktʃu.eɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-actuate.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-actuate.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[activate]], or to put into [[motion]]; to [[animate]].
#* '''November 6, 1750''', {{w|Samuel Johnson}}, ''The Rambler'' No. 67
#*: Wings, which others were contriving to '''actuate''' by the perpetual motion.
#* {{RQ:Conrad Secret Agent|chapter=IV|pages=68–69|pageref=69| passage=I walk always with my right hand closed round the india-rubber ball which I have in my trouser pocket. The pressing of this ball '''actuates''' a detonator inside the flask I carry in my pocket. It's the principle of the pneumatic instantaneous shutter for a camera lens.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[incite]] to [[action]]; to [[motivate]].
#* '''1748''', {{w|David Hume}}, ''Enquiries concerning the human understanding and concerning the principles of moral'', 2. ed., London, Oxford University Press, 1973. § 11.
#*: A man in a fit of anger, is '''actuated''' in a very different manner from one who only thinks of that emotion.
#* {{RQ:Spectator|III|255|22 December 1711}}
#*: Men of the greatest abilities are most fired with ambition; and, on the contrary, mean and narrow minds are the least '''actuated''' by it.

